# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Tclunk tag[2] fid[4]
size[4] Rclunk tag[2]
```

# DESCRIPTION

The `clunk` request, type 120, tells the server that <fid> is no longer needed; `Rclunk` is type 121.
Once clunked, the fid may be used again in a walk or an attach.

A VectraOS server answers a <fid> the connection does not have with `unknown fid`; any other is let go and answered `Rclunk`, which then cannot fail.
The file server is told the fid let its file go, and whether the fid was open, and some act on it: fsd(4) removes a file removed while open when its last fid goes, and dosfs(4) writes out a file written through the fid.

With the `posix` extension, an open fid has an open file the server keeps, perhaps shared with fids on other connections (see share(5)).
The clunk takes the fid from it; the open file goes when it has no fids left and no holds for `Tjoin`, and holds outlive its last fid by 10 seconds.
Clunking an open fid also lets go of every lock the connection holds on the file, whatever the lock's process (see lock(5)), as POSIX's `close` lets go of a process's locks on a file.

The server clunks fids itself as well: `Tremove` clunks its fid whether the remove works or not (see remove(5)), `Tversion` clunks every fid on the connection (see version(5)), and so does the connection's end.

VectraOS's client clunks a fid when the program is done with it, and the fid a walk of a long path made when a later step fails; it does not use a fid's number again.

# SEE ALSO

attach(5), walk(5), open(5), remove(5), version(5)

# BUGS

A file opened with `ORCLOSE` is removed at its fid's clunk, or its open file's last (see open(5)), whether or not the removal is allowed by then: the permission was checked at the open, as Plan 9 has it; a removal that fails is not reported.
